math_ii_linear_algebra_cardinality_of_the_continuum_47ab9b24_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"To show that the open interval $(0,1)$ has cardinality $\\mathfrak{c}=\\operatorname{card}(\\mathbb{R})$, one looks for a bijection $(0,1)\\to\\mathbb{R}$. Which of the following formulas, with $x$ ranging over $(0,1)$, defines such a bijection?","options":["$x\\mapsto\\tan(\\pi x)$","$x\\mapsto\\tan\\left(\\pi x-\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$","$x\\mapsto\\tan x$","$x\\mapsto\\tan\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}x\\right)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"49dcefc9ac1943dee656ec5d","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_cardinality_of_the_continuum_47ab9b24_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\mathfrak{c}=\\operatorname{card}(\\mathbb{R})$. How do the cardinalities of the plane $\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{R}$ and of the power set $2^{\\mathbb{R}}$ compare with $\\mathfrak{c}$?","options":["$\\operatorname{card}(\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{R})>\\mathfrak{c}$ and $\\operatorname{card}(2^{\\mathbb{R}})>\\mathfrak{c}$","$\\operatorname{card}(\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{R})=\\mathfrak{c}$ and $\\operatorname{card}(2^{\\mathbb{R}})=\\mathfrak{c}$","$\\operatorname{card}(\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{R})>\\mathfrak{c}$ and $\\operatorname{card}(2^{\\mathbb{R}})=\\mathfrak{c}$","$\\operatorname{card}(\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{R})=\\mathfrak{c}$ and $\\operatorname{card}(2^{\\mathbb{R}})>\\mathfrak{c}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1a3cccc3809b708b0ebe727f","time_limit":30}]}}